# Surplomb — environment variables
# Copy to .env and fill in your keys. On macOS the launcher can also read keys
# from the Keychain (see README); on Linux/Windows use this file or env vars.
#
# NOTE ON CLIENT-EXPOSED KEYS: GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY, CESIUM_ION_TOKEN and
# ARCGIS_API_KEY are injected into the browser bundle by design (they're used
# client-side) and WILL be visible in devtools. Restrict/scope them rather than
# trying to hide them (see SECURITY.md). All other keys below stay server-side.

# Required: Google Maps API key (Map Tiles API must be enabled).
# CLIENT-EXPOSED — restrict it (HTTP referrer + API restriction) in Google Cloud.
# WORTH KNOWING IF YOUR BILLING ADDRESS IS IN THE EEA: Google withholds
# Photorealistic 3D Tiles and satellite imagery from EEA-billed projects (403
# PERMISSION_DENIED, see developers.google.com/maps/comms/eea/map-tiles), but
# it still serves the 2D roadmap and terrain basemaps on the same key. So the
# "Google 3D" map source will be greyed out while "Plan Google" and "Relief
# Google" work. `scripts/set-google-key.sh --check` probes a key and tells you
# which of the two cases you are in.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=your_google_maps_api_key_here
# Optional, and only worth it on a PUBLIC origin: a SECOND Google key for the
# browser bundle, so the key anyone can read out of devtools is not the same
# one the server proxies spend. `deploy/vps/docker-compose.yml` passes this one
# as the build arg and leaves GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY above as runtime env only.
# Restrict this one to your site's referrers and to Map Tiles + Geocoding;
# restrict the one above to your server's IP and to Places + Street View +
# Map Tiles. A referrer restriction on a single shared key cannot work: the
# server sends no `Referer`, so /api/google/* would 403 on every call.
# Unset, the build falls back to GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY and nothing changes.
GOOGLE_MAPS_BROWSER_KEY=
# Optional: opt-in per-IP rate limit for the Google Places cost endpoint
# (/api/google/nearby-places), in requests per minute.
# DEFAULT IS UNLIMITED — unset (or 0) means no throttling, unchanged behavior.
# Set a positive integer to cap per-IP Places quota spend when self-hosting.
# Recommended whenever HOST is not localhost. NOTE: this is a per-IP,
# process-local guard (in-memory, resets on restart; over-limit requests get a
# sanitized 429) — it is NOT a billing cap. For hard spend protection set
# provider-side budget alerts: Google Cloud Console -> Billing -> Budgets & alerts
# (plus per-API quotas under APIs & Services -> Quotas).
# GEV_RATELIMIT_GOOGLE_PER_MIN=60
#
# Optional: the SAME endpoint, capped across ALL callers instead of per IP.
# Per-IP is fairness; this one is the bill. Unset, it defaults to 20x the
# per-IP cap — so a "prudent" 60/min above silently authorises 1200 Places
# calls a minute. Set this before removing GEV_ACCESS_PASSWORD.
# GEV_RATELIMIT_GOOGLE_GLOBAL_PER_MIN=120

# Optional: Cesium ion token for Bing world imagery stacks, Cesium World Terrain,
# and Google's Photorealistic 3D Tiles (ion asset 2275207, served under Cesium's
# own Google contract). That last one is the ONLY route to the 3D globe when the
# Google key above is billed to an EEA address: Google answers 403 there for 3D
# and satellite while still serving roadmap and terrain on the same key. The free
# ion tier is personal/non-commercial and adds an "Upgrade for commercial use."
# link to the on-screen credits.
# CLIENT-EXPOSED — use a public assets:read token with URL restrictions.
CESIUM_ION_TOKEN=

# Optional: ArcGIS Location Platform API key for the world satellite base under
# the Satellite stack — Esri World Imagery, licensed and billed per tile (2M
# free a month, then $0.15 per 1,000; no subscription). Without it the base is
# Esri's anonymous endpoint, which Esri does not make available for commercial
# use: fine for a clone, switched off with GEV_NONCOMMERCIAL_SOURCES=off below,
# where the base falls back to Sentinel-2 cloudless 2016.
# CLIENT-EXPOSED and inlined at build time — restrict it to your site's
# referrers and to the Basemaps privilege only. Steps in docs/DEPLOY.md.
ARCGIS_API_KEY=

# Optional: OpenAI Realtime voice control. Do not prefix with VITE_.
# On this fork the key alone is not enough: GEV_VOICE_PROVIDER below decides
# which brain the mic uses, and a named provider beats a present key. So a key
# dropped here while GEV_VOICE_PROVIDER=openrouter changes nothing you can hear.
# `scripts/set-openai-key.sh` probes a key against the realtime endpoint the app
# actually calls, then writes both lines together, here and in the seed .env.
#
# BUDGET, not price. A realtime response re-sends the whole session prefix —
# instructions plus all 29 tool schemas — which is ~10 900 input tokens before
# anyone speaks. An entry-tier OpenAI account is capped at 40 000 tokens per
# MINUTE, i.e. three responses, and one spoken command that calls a tool is two
# of them. Past that the mic answers `failed` and the dock says
# "TOKEN LIMIT REACHED"; the app waits out the window and retries the swallowed
# turn once. Raise the ceiling at
# platform.openai.com/settings/organization/limits — no code change helps as much.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_REALTIME_MODEL=gpt-realtime-2
# Cheaper voice tier, used when the GEV MIC panel's MINI toggle is on.
# Model ids and prices are external facts that drift — if OpenAI moves this id,
# override it here rather than editing src/voice/voiceCost.js.
OPENAI_REALTIME_MODEL_MINI=gpt-realtime-2.1-mini
OPENAI_REALTIME_VOICE=marin
OPENAI_REALTIME_REASONING_EFFORT=low
# Keep only a short conversational window. Current map state is fetched live.
OPENAI_REALTIME_CONTEXT_TOKENS=3000
OPENAI_REALTIME_CONTEXT_RETENTION=0.5
OPENAI_HUD_SUMMARY_MODEL=gpt-5-nano
# Optional: opt-in per-IP rate limit for the OpenAI cost endpoints
# (/api/realtime/token + /api/openai/hud-summary), in requests per minute.
# DEFAULT IS UNLIMITED — unset (or 0) means no throttling, unchanged behavior.
# Set a positive integer to cap per-IP spend when self-hosting on a network.
# Recommended whenever HOST is not localhost. NOTE: this is a per-IP,
# process-local guard (in-memory, resets on restart; over-limit requests get a
# sanitized 429) — it is NOT a billing cap. For hard spend protection set a
# provider-side budget: OpenAI platform -> Settings -> Limits (usage limits).
# GEV_RATELIMIT_OPENAI_PER_MIN=30
#
# Optional: the SAME endpoints, capped across ALL callers instead of per IP.
# A caller with a hundred addresses has a hundred per-IP buckets; it has one
# of these. Unset, it defaults to 20x the per-IP cap. Once it trips everybody
# gets a 429 and the page degrades (no HUD summary) — which is the intended
# failure, and it is the knob that makes a public origin affordable.
# GEV_RATELIMIT_OPENAI_GLOBAL_PER_MIN=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOSTED TRIAL + WAITLIST — off unless GEV_TRIAL_LIMIT is set. Leave it unset
# on your own machine: a clone running on its own keys owes nobody a waitlist.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tries per browser at the keyed comfort routes (one HUD summary = one try;
# nearby places and text search are refused once the trial is spent). The
# count lives in a signed, HttpOnly cookie — a cleared cookie starts over, so
# this is fairness between visitors, NOT a spending bound: the *_GLOBAL_PER_MIN
# caps above and the provider-side limits are the bound.
# GEV_TRIAL_LIMIT=5
#
# HMAC key for that cookie. Unset, a random one is made at boot and every
# restart gives every visitor a fresh trial. Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
# GEV_TRIAL_SECRET=
#
# Spoken requests in the voice trial. Opening it spends ONE of the tries above,
# and it never comes back: 3 requests per browser, not 3 per try. A realtime
# session spends them all when it is minted and the page closes it after the
# last answer, then shows the premium card. `0` keeps voice out of the trial
# (the mic opens the card straight away). Capped at 20.
# GEV_TRIAL_VOICE=3
#
# Buttondown username for the waitlist form (the card posts to
# https://buttondown.com/api/emails/embed-subscribe/<username>). Unset, the
# card explains the trial but has no form. No price on the card: it belongs on
# the payment page that comes later.
# GEV_WAITLIST_BUTTONDOWN=
#
# The operator's own browser, without a trial. Set, `node scripts/owner-pass.mjs
# [origin]` (run where this file is read — `docker exec gev …` on the VPS)
# prints a single-use link, valid ten minutes; its button writes a signed
# `gev_owner` cookie (400 days, the site and its www. name) that is never
# counted nor refused. The *_GLOBAL_PER_MIN caps still apply. Rotate the value
# to revoke every pass. Under 32 characters it is ignored. Unset, the route is
# a 404. Generate: openssl rand -base64 48
# GEV_OWNER_PASS_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOSTED WELCOME-CARD A/B TEST — off unless GEV_FIRST_RUN_AB names at least two
# variants. Leave it unset on your own machine: a clone measures nobody.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The first-run card variants to draw from, comma-separated (A « Une adresse »,
# B « Trois questions », C no card, a bubble on the search field). Set, it
# switches on, together and per request: the draw in the browser (equal
# shares, kept in localStorage for at most 13 months), at most two reports per
# visit to POST /api/first-run/events, the day files that store them, the
# `experiments` field of /api/trial, `"abtest": true` on /healthz, and the
# paragraphs of /confidentialite that describe the test and its opt-out.
# Rollback = remove the line and recreate the container (`docker compose up -d`,
# no rebuild): every browser shows A again and forgets its draw on its next
# visit. Reading the results: `node scripts/first-run-ab-report.mjs <dir>`.
# GEV_FIRST_RUN_AB=A,B,C
#
# Optional: where the reports are written, one JSONL file per UTC day, swept
# after 90 days. Default `.gev-cache/first-run-ab` — the volume a redeploy keeps.
# GEV_FIRST_RUN_AB_DIR=

# Optional: doctors' names (layer Médecins FR). They are never in git: the
# layer shows them only after `npm run medecins:registry` has built the pair
# into this directory (default `.gev-cache/medecins-fr/pack`, the volume a
# redeploy keeps). Without it the layer draws every practice, unnamed.
# GEV_MEDECINS_PACK_DIR=
#
# Optional: the doctors who asked not to appear, one per line (see
# src/data/local_data/medecins_fr/README.md). Default
# `.gev-cache/medecins-fr/suppress.txt`; read again whenever it changes.
# GEV_MEDECINS_SUPPRESS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SOURCES FOR NON-COMMERCIAL USE ONLY — kept unless you switch them off.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Some free sources may be used for non-commercial purposes only (today:
# Open-Meteo for the cockpit's weather, Esri's anonymous World Imagery endpoint
# for the satellite base beyond France, OpenSky for the live flights, Google
# News RSS for the cockpit's headlines, the TeleGeography cable map), and
# Google's EEA terms keep Street View stills off a page with a non-Google map
# (the CCTV fallback frame). Running the globe for yourself is fine; a hosted
# site run by a business is not. `off` turns every source listed in
# src/nonCommercialSources.js off, per request: no weather, Sentinel-2 beyond
# France unless ARCGIS_API_KEY is set, flights from adsb.lol only (four 250 NM
# circles over France, one circle around the view elsewhere), headlines from
# GDELT alone, « IMAGE · INDISPONIBLE » for a dead camera, no cable layer (the
# server refuses its files), and none of their credits.
# Unset or `on` keeps them; any other value turns them off. Check with
# `curl -s <host>/healthz | jq .sourcesOff`.
# GEV_NONCOMMERCIAL_SOURCES=off

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VOICE PROVIDER — pick which brain drives the mic.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# auto (default) : use whichever key below is set; OpenAI wins when both are.
# openai         : the speech-to-speech Realtime session (needs OPENAI_API_KEY).
# openrouter     : browser speech recognition + browser speech synthesis, with
#                  ONE text model in the middle (needs OPENROUTER_API_KEY).
#                  Turn-based, not full duplex: you cannot interrupt it
#                  mid-sentence the way you can interrupt the Realtime model.
# off            : no mic at all, even with keys present.
# Naming a provider whose key is missing turns voice OFF and says so on the
# dock, rather than silently billing the other account.
# GEV_VOICE_PROVIDER=auto

# Language the mic listens and speaks in (BCP-47). Drives browser speech
# recognition, voice selection for speech synthesis, and one instruction telling
# the model which language to SPEAK. The tool contract stays English either way.
# GEV_VOICE_LANGUAGE=en-US

# Optional: OpenRouter voice brain — an alternative to OPENAI_API_KEY, not an
# addition to it. One key covers every model OpenRouter fronts, so this is the
# cheapest way to run the mic without an OpenAI account. Do not prefix with VITE_.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# The default is Mistral Medium 3.1, chosen on a French routing bench over the
# app's own 28 tool schemas: 26/26 on tool choice, and the only model in that
# bench that refused to invent history when handed a thin source document.
# Any OpenRouter model with tool-calling works here.
OPENROUTER_VOICE_MODEL=mistralai/mistral-medium-3.1
# Optional, cosmetic: how this install appears on the OpenRouter dashboard.
# OPENROUTER_SITE_URL=https://your.host
# OPENROUTER_APP_NAME=Surplomb
# Optional: opt-in per-IP rate limit for /api/voice/brain, in requests per
# minute. DEFAULT IS UNLIMITED. The server holds the key and owns the system
# prompt and tool list, but a public instance should still cap this — every
# request is a billable model call. For hard spend protection set a credit
# limit on the OpenRouter key itself (Keys -> edit -> credit limit).
# GEV_RATELIMIT_VOICE_BRAIN_PER_MIN=20
#
# Optional: the same route, capped across ALL callers. Unset it defaults to
# 20x the per-IP cap. Every request here is a billable model call.
# GEV_RATELIMIT_VOICE_BRAIN_GLOBAL_PER_MIN=40

# Optional: OpenSky auth mode (oauth | basic | auto | anon)
# Default: oauth — requires client credentials below
# Set to "anon" to skip aircraft auth (rate-limited but works)
OPENSKY_AUTH_MODE=oauth

# Optional: OpenSky OAuth credentials (from opensky-network.org account dashboard)
OPENSKY_CLIENT_ID=
OPENSKY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Optional: OpenSky credentials JSON file path (alternative to above)
# OPENSKY_CREDENTIALS_FILE=/path/to/credentials.json

# Optional: RTE data portal — per-unit electricity generation for France
# (layer "Groupes de prod (FR)"). FREE account, no card, no quota to speak of:
#   1. create an account at https://data.rte-france.com/create_account
#   2. create an application and attach the "Actual Generation" API to it
#   3. copy the client id / secret pair below
# WITHOUT these the layer still draws the whole 171-unit, 93.5 GW French fleet
# from the register it ships — names, filière, installed power, position. The
# credential adds one thing: what each machine is actually producing.
RTE_CLIENT_ID=
RTE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The RTE dashboard also shows the pair already base64-encoded. If you copied
# THAT, paste it here instead and leave the two above empty.
# RTE_BASE64_KEY=

# Optional: hosted deployment (see docs/DEPLOY.md).
# Set a password and the whole origin — app shell, assets and every /api
# proxy — sits behind HTTP Basic. Leave it empty for local dev; set it for
# ANY reachable deployment, because each proxy here brokers a key you pay for.
# GEV_ACCESS_USER=gev
# GEV_ACCESS_PASSWORD=
# Hostname(s) the deployment answers on, comma-separated. `vite preview`
# rejects unknown Host headers otherwise ("Blocked request").
# GEV_PUBLIC_HOST=gev.example.com
# Behind a reverse proxy or Cloudflare tunnel, the header that carries the real
# client address (`cf-connecting-ip` for Cloudflare, `x-real-ip` for nginx).
# Without it every visitor shares ONE per-IP throttle bucket, because the
# socket peer is the proxy. Opt-in: only set it when nothing can reach this
# port except through that proxy — a direct caller could forge the header.
# Check with `curl /healthz` → `client` must be your public address.
# GEV_TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=cf-connecting-ip
#
# The publisher of a PUBLISHED instance (French law, LCEN art. 1-1), shown on
# /mentions-legales and /confidentialite, and named as the seller on /cgv (the
# terms of sale of the paid offer). Read per request, never committed and
# never built into the bundle: edit, recreate the container, done. Unset, the
# pages say the instance has not named its publisher, and `/healthz` answers
# `"legal": false`. REGISTRATION is the RCS/SIREN line, optional for an
# unregistered individual. PHONE is optional here but the law asks for one:
# leaving it out is the operator's call. HOSTING lists every provider that
# hosts or stores what the site processes, separated by `|`.
# GEV_LEGAL_PUBLISHER=
# GEV_LEGAL_REGISTRATION=
# GEV_LEGAL_ADDRESS=
# GEV_LEGAL_PHONE=
# GEV_LEGAL_EMAIL=
# GEV_LEGAL_DIRECTOR=
# GEV_LEGAL_HOSTING=Hébergeur, adresse, téléphone | Stockage, adresse
# A hosted server writes no voice debug log (the transcript of a visitor's
# session). Set to 1 for a debugging session only, then remove it.
# GEV_REALTIME_DEBUG_LOG=1

# Optional: dev server config
PORT=4173
# Network binding. Default is localhost — the server is reachable ONLY from
# this machine. This is the safe default: the dev server brokers your
# configured API keys, so anyone who can reach it can spend your quota.
# LAN opt-in: set HOST=0.0.0.0 to share on your local network. WARNING: that
# exposes every key-brokering proxy to everyone on the network — use only on
# networks you trust, and consider the GEV_RATELIMIT_* throttles above.
# HOST=localhost
# HOST=0.0.0.0

# Optional: NASA FIRMS live active fires. SERVER-SIDE ONLY (never reaches the
# browser — the client polls the /api/firms proxy). Free key:
# https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/api/map_key/
# Without a key the FIRMS Active Fires layer is empty ("KEY REQUIRED").
# FIRMS_MAP_KEY=

# Optional: Bison Futé "Action b" — road events on the CONCEDED motorways
# (ASF, APRR, Sanef…) for the Événements routiers layer. SERVER-SIDE ONLY.
# Requires a signed reuse licence: https://www.bison-fute.gouv.fr/directive-sti,id_sous_rubrique10402.html
# The login is personal to the licence holder; never commit it. Without it the
# layer draws the State-run network from the open feed.
# BISON_FUTE_RESTRICTED_USER=
# BISON_FUTE_RESTRICTED_PASSWORD=

# Optional: Launch Library 2 token — a higher request allowance for the space
# missions layer. The layer works on public access without it.
# Get one at https://thespacedevs.com
# LL2_API_TOKEN=

# Optional: Live AIS vessels layer
# Browser fetch path. Keep same-origin so the private stream key stays server-side.
VITE_AIS_LIVE_API_URL=/api/ais-live
# Client render cap. The server can keep a larger AISStream cache, but drawing
# every global vessel at once can overwhelm WebGL on laptops.
VITE_AIS_LIVE_MAX_ROWS=12000
# Active label cap after screen-space clustering. Labels exist for all rendered
# ships, but only clustered winners are shown to keep text readable.
VITE_AIS_LIVE_LABEL_MAX_ROWS=900
# Private AISStream key for the server-side websocket. Do not prefix with VITE_.
AISSTREAM_API_KEY=
# Optional AISStream subscription filters.
# The DEFAULT is metropolitan France and its approaches — [[[41,-8],[51.6,10]]]
# — not the planet. AIS carries the type, name, IMO and hull only in a message
# sent every ~6 minutes, and on the world box those identity messages lose the
# race against ~18 300 contacts' worth of position reports; over France there
# are ~3 750 (measured 2026-09-03), so the declared-type share climbs instead of
# flatlining. The box is METROPOLITAN: the DOM-TOM are not in it. Uncomment the
# line below to get the whole world back, or list several boxes to add regions.
# AISSTREAM_BOUNDING_BOXES=[[[-90,-180],[90,180]]]
# AISSTREAM_MESSAGE_TYPES=PositionReport,StandardClassBPositionReport,ExtendedClassBPositionReport,ShipStaticData,StaticDataReport
# Feed watchdog: silence (ms) after which the feed is REPORTED stale; the socket
# is recycled at 2.5x that. Default 120000. The watchdog self-arms for the two
# subscriptions whose message volume has been measured — the France default and
# the world box, both unfiltered. A narrower box or a shortened message-type
# list can be legitimately quiet, so set this explicitly (sized for your filter)
# to opt back in. 0 disables the silence watch entirely.
# AISSTREAM_SILENCE_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# Learned vessel identities (MMSI -> type, name, IMO, hull) are cached in
# .gev-cache/ais-static/registry.json for 30 days, so a restart no longer has
# to re-learn the fleet six minutes at a time. Delete the file to start over.

# Optional: Météo-France Vigilance. SERVER-SIDE ONLY (never reaches the browser
# — the client polls the /api/vigilance proxy).
# THE LAYER WORKS WITHOUT THIS KEY. Météo-France mirrors the identical
# vigilance product on data.gouv.fr under Licence Ouverte 2.0, with a measured
# ~20-second publication lag, and the proxy uses that mirror by default. Set a
# key only if you want the contracted API (99.9% availability, 60 req/min)
# instead; the proxy falls back to the mirror if the key ever fails.
# Get one at https://portail-api.meteofrance.fr — the portal's "clé API" is a
# long-lived string sent as an `apikey:` header, which is simpler to operate
# than its OAuth2 alternative.
# METEOFRANCE_API_KEY=

# Optional: TomTom live traffic flow (BYOK freemium — the free tier is 200,000
# flow-tile requests per MONTH, https://docs.tomtom.com/pricing; get a key at
# https://developer.tomtom.com). SERVER-SIDE ONLY:
# the browser fetches same-origin /api/tomtom/* and never sees the key.
# Keyless fallback: the traffic layer runs its built-in simulation (white dots,
# hardcoded per-road-class speeds) — no key required for the layer to work.
# TOMTOM_API_KEY=
# Optional cap on upstream tile fetches per UTC day. The default, 6451, is the
# free monthly allowance spread over 31 days; raise it on a paid plan, lower it
# if the key is shared with another project. Over the cap the proxy serves
# cached/stale tiles, or answers 429 until midnight UTC, instead of hitting
# upstream.
# TOMTOM_DAILY_TILE_BUDGET=6451

# Optional: CCTV layer tuning (advanced). Defaults are sensible — leave unset
# unless you're customizing the camera source pack.
# CCTV_SOURCES_FILE=config/cctv_sources.austin.json   # path to a source-pack JSON
# CCTV_SOURCES_JSON=                                   # inline source-pack JSON (overrides file)
# CCTV_PACKS_DIR=config/cctv-packs                    # folder of camera packs ADDED to the live packs (see its README)
# CCTV_PACKS_DISABLED=                                 # comma-separated pack ids to skip
# CCTV_PACKS_ENABLED=1                                 # 0 skips every camera pack
# CCTV_AUSTIN_ROWS_URL=                                # override the Austin Open Data catalog URL
# CCTV_AUSTIN_MAX_SOURCES=36                           # cap cameras loaded from the Austin catalog
# CCTV_LYON_ENABLED=1                                  # Métropole de Lyon (Criter) camera pack — 0 disables
# CCTV_LYON_MAX_SOURCES=60                             # cap cameras loaded from the Grand Lyon catalog
# CCTV_MAX_SOURCES=48                                  # overall cap on registered cameras
# CCTV_PREFER_AUSTIN=1                                 # prioritize Austin cameras in the registry
# CCTV_FORCE_AUSTIN=                                   # force Austin-only sources
# CCTV_AUTO_CALIBRATE=1                                # auto-calibrate camera projection (client flag)
# CCTV_DRAPE_MESH=1                                    # drape frames onto 3D geometry (client flag)

# Optional: the camera timelapse. Lyon's Criter cameras publish only their
# latest still, so the server records one frame a minute per camera (shrunk to
# 640 px, ~45 KB) under `.gev-cache/cctv-timelapse/` and the camera panel plays
# the last hour back. Recording pulls ~0.42 MB per camera per minute from the
# Métropole — ~7.3 GB/day for the ~12 working cameras — for ~30 MB of disk.
# CCTV_TIMELAPSE=on-demand          # always | on-demand (record for WARM_MIN after anyone opens a Lyon camera) | off
# CCTV_TIMELAPSE_WINDOW_MIN=60      # minutes kept and played back (5..360)
# CCTV_TIMELAPSE_WARM_MIN=180       # on-demand only: minutes recording continues after the last look

# Optional: OSM mapped cameras — publicly mapped surveillance-camera POSITIONS
# (man_made=surveillance) from OpenStreetMap via Overpass (ODbL 1.0, keyless),
# loaded for the viewport you are looking at (plus a snapped margin) and merged
# into the CCTV layer. OFF BY DEFAULT and opt-in on purpose: OSM maps where a
# camera IS, never what it sees, so these cameras carry NO feed and every frame
# falls back to a BILLABLE Google Street View still (or the synthetic
# "NO UPSTREAM CONFIGURED" placeholder without a Maps key). Poses are modeled
# priors from tags and stay RAW PRIOR until calibrated. No country limit: it
# answers wherever OSM has mapped cameras.
# CCTV_OSM_CAMERAS_ENABLED=1                           # enable the source (only "1" enables it)

# Optional: the chronicle — the recorder for the five French feeds that publish
# only the present and whose past nobody archives (GTFS-RT, QualiCharge dynamic
# charge-point status, Bison Futé DATEX II, AIS over the France box, Vigicrues).
# It folds each into a typical week (kept for ever, a few hundred KB a source)
# and keeps thirty days of raw ticks under `.gev-cache/chronicle/`. Read it back
# at /api/chronicle-fr/status. See docs/CHRONICLE.md.
#
# Four of the five ride on fetches the proxies already make, so they cost
# nothing upstream and are ON by default.
# CHRONICLE_DISABLED=1          # turn the whole recorder off (nothing is written)
#
# The fifth is a poller of its own — 1.17 MB gzipped every 15 min, ~112 MB/day
# against transport.data.gouv.fr's proxy — so it is OPT-IN. Worth arming on a
# deployment with a persistent volume; pointless on a laptop whose .gev-cache
# is thrown away. The staging compose file sets it (deploy/vps/docker-compose.yml).
# CHRONICLE_IRVE_DYNAMIC=1
#
# The communes' Cart@DS permit boards keep about two months, so the server
# keeps every row it reads under `.gev-cache/archive/cartds/` and sweeps every
# board once a day (~270 requests, one second apart). ON under `vite preview`,
# OFF under `vite dev`; this overrides both. `npm run cartds:archive` runs the
# same sweep from a shell.
